Purchase-price allocation assigns acquisition consideration to identifiable assets and liabilities; residual goodwill follows the applicable combination rules.|Pay ₹120 for identifiable net assets worth ₹100: simplified goodwill ₹20.|Revalued acquired assets can change future…?|Depreciation and amortisation|Only the purchase date|Only the buyer's logo
Goodwill is an acquisition residual; impairment tests assess recoverability under the relevant framework.|Goodwill ₹20 with recognised impairment ₹5 falls to ₹15.|Does impairment itself require a new ₹5 cash payment?|No|Yes|Only if the acquisition was cash-funded
Subsidiaries involve control; associates significant influence; joint ventures joint control with rights to net assets under IFRS definitions.|A controlled investee is generally consolidated; an associate generally uses the equity method.|Classification depends only on a universal ownership percentage?|No|Yes|Only for foreign entities
Consolidation combines controlled entities and eliminates intragroup balances, transactions, and relevant unrealised profits.|An internal inventory sale with ₹3 unrealised profit requires elimination while stock remains within the group.|Can internal sales inflate group revenue legitimately?|No|Yes|Only if invoiced
Equity-method investments reflect the investor's share of relevant investee results and other changes rather than line-by-line consolidation.|A 30% associate earns ₹10: simplified investor share of profit ₹3.|Dividends from that already-recognised profit generally…?|Reduce the investment balance|Create the same profit again|Increase inventory
Lease accounting recognises use rights and obligations under applicable rules; IFRS 16 lessees generally recognise assets and liabilities, subject to exemptions.|Lease liability ₹100 with ₹10 interest and ₹30 payment closes at ₹80.|Capitalising a lease makes its economic cash payments disappear?|No|Yes|Only for property
Financial-instrument classification determines amortised cost, fair value through OCI, or profit-and-loss treatment under applicable criteria.|IFRS 9 debt classification considers business model and contractual cash-flow characteristics.|Measurement classification is chosen only by management's preferred profit?|No|Yes|Only if held long term
Hedge accounting aligns qualifying hedge effects with hedged items when designation and other requirements are met.|A qualifying forecast-purchase cash-flow hedge may initially place effective changes in OCI.|Any economic hedge automatically qualifies for hedge accounting?|No|Yes|Only if profitable
Foreign transactions and foreign-operation translation use different accounting processes and relevant exchange rates.|A foreign-currency payable can generate transaction gains or losses as rates change.|Transaction and translation exposure are always identical?|No|Yes|Only for subsidiaries
Share-based compensation records employee services paid through equity or cash-linked awards under relevant measurement rules.|Recognise award expense as qualifying employee service is received, with corresponding equity or liability.|An equity award is costless because no immediate cash leaves?|No|Yes|Only if unvested
Defined-contribution expense relates to required contributions; defined-benefit accounting estimates obligations, assets, and relevant remeasurements.|A benefit obligation ₹100 and plan assets ₹80 imply a simplified ₹20 deficit.|The obligation can change with actuarial assumptions?|Yes|No|Only when benefits are paid
Deferred tax incorporates temporary differences; loss-related tax assets require applicable recognition and recoverability conditions.|An unused tax loss may support an asset only to the extent recognition criteria are satisfied.|Every tax loss automatically creates a fully recoverable asset?|No|Yes|Only after filing
Long-term contracts recognise revenue over time only when applicable criteria are met; progress measures and contract balances require judgment.|A qualifying ₹100 contract at 40% measured progress may recognise ₹40 revenue, subject to the contract's facts.|Time spent alone always proves over-time recognition?|No|Yes|Only for construction
Securitisation packages cash-flow claims; derecognition depends on applicable transfer, risk, reward, and control criteria.|Selling receivables with retained significant risk may leave assets recognised with financing.|A legal sale necessarily removes all accounting exposure?|No|Yes|Only if cash is received
Related-party transactions involve connected persons or entities and can create conflicts; examine terms and disclosures.|A company lends to its controlling owner's entity below market terms.|The main analytical concern is…?|Economic substance and conflicts|Only transaction size|Only whether paperwork exists
Restructuring, disposal, and discontinued-operation accounting follows specific recognition and presentation conditions.|An announced plan alone may not establish a qualifying restructuring provision.|Can any management intention create a provision immediately?|No|Yes|Only at year-end
